Adrenal Crisis
Acute Urgent Care
Refractory hypotension, Severe weakness, Hyponatraemia/Hyperkalaemia, Low serum cortisol

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-016
Reason for Visit: Sudden, severe, unexplained physical collapse, vomiting, and profound weakness requiring immediate clinical stabilization.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage described a patient with a known history of Addison's disease presenting with profound, unremitting weakness, vomiting, and pre-syncopal symptoms following a mild gastrointestinal illness. Urgent, immediate face-to-face assessment and administration of parenteral corticosteroids were required to arrest an evolving adrenal crisis, as delaying intervention until routine in-hours care would risk irreversible hypovolemic shock, coma, and death.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I can't even lift my head off the pillow, my whole body aches, and I feel completely drained of life.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a 24-hour history of severe asthenia and generalized weakness that escalated sharply over the last 6 hours. This followed a minor presumed viral gastroenteritis yesterday, which caused two episodes of vomiting and anorexia. The patient now reports generalized, unexplained abdominal pain and severe dizziness upon attempting to sit up. The spouse notes that the patient has become increasingly lethargic and confused over the past 2 hours. Despite oral fluid intake, the patient feels profoundly dehydrated and weak.
Functional Status: Bedbound, unable to stand without presyncope.
Social/Safety Concerns: Rapidly deteriorating mental status; high risk of complete cardiovascular collapse.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 85/50 | HR: 118 | Temp: 38.2 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient appears markedly lethargic, pale, and profoundly dehydrated with dry mucous membranes.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals resting tachycardia and profound hypotension, which remains refractory to initial attempts at oral fluid resuscitation. Neurological exam reveals an altered mental status characterized by delayed responsiveness and somnolence. Abdominal exam demonstrates diffuse tenderness without rebound or guarding. Inspection of the oral cavity reveals hyperpigmentation of the buccal mucosa consistent with primary adrenal failure. Expected laboratory investigations would demonstrate profound hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia, accompanied by a critically low morning serum cortisol and failure to respond to a synthetic ACTH stimulation test.
Environmental Observations: Vomit bowl at the bedside; room is darkened due to patient photophobia/headache.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient missed their regular morning and evening dose of oral Hydrocortisone due to vomiting.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Adrenal Crisis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Septic Shock - Ruled out/unlikely due to the known history of Addison's disease and the absence of a primary focal infectious source (e.g., clear lungs, no urinary symptoms), though concurrent infection is a recognized trigger.

Severe Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the profound degree of shock and altered mental status being disproportionate to the mild preceding gastrointestinal symptoms, pointing directly to endocrine failure.

Diabetic Ketoacidosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to normal capillary blood glucose (5.4 mmol/L) and no history of Type 1 Diabetes, eliminating osmotic diuresis as the cause of hypovolemia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The diagnostic tetrad of refractory hypotension, severe weakness, suspected severe electrolyte derangement, and presumed critically low serum cortisol perfectly aligns with an acute adrenal crisis triggered by intercurrent illness and missed oral steroid doses. The patient is already exhibiting signs of advanced hypovolemic shock and impending coma. Urgent after-hours intervention was life-saving; failing to physically assess the patient and immediately administer parenteral hydrocortisone before morning hours would have definitively resulted in fatal cardiovascular collapse.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Hydrocortisone 100mg Intramuscularly (IM) immediately. Placed patient flat with legs elevated. Called 000 for Priority 1 ambulance transport for IV fluid resuscitation (Normal Saline).
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly implementing your "sick day rules" and doubling your oral steroid dose the moment you feel a fever or illness coming on, you give your body the essential stress hormones it needs to fight the infection without crashing into a crisis.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ambulance transport.
DME/Safety (Optional): Advised keeping an emergency IM Hydrocortisone injection kit at home.
Follow-up: Endocrinology review post-hospital discharge.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. Should the patient lose consciousness or cease breathing prior to paramedic arrival, the spouse must initiate CPR immediately and follow the 000 dispatcher's instructions.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Your body has completely run out of cortisol, a vital stress hormone. You need immediate hospital treatment to replace massive amounts of fluid and steroids.
Clinical Course: With prompt administration of high-dose intravenous steroids and saline, your blood pressure and mental clarity will improve significantly over the next few hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to sit up or stand, as your blood pressure is dangerously low and you will faint. Keep your legs elevated on these pillows to help blood reach your brain.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: I have given you a massive dose of cortisol directly into your muscle. This is the hormone your body cannot make on its own, and it is the only thing that will tell your blood vessels to tighten up and raise your blood pressure.
Actionable Steps: Try to take slow, steady breaths. Your spouse will pack your regular steroid medications to show the hospital doctors exactly what doses you normally take.

